27.3 5. groupby is MapReduce
You have been writing MapReduce jobs since Session 2. df.groupby("city")["amount"].sum() is a map (pick out city and amount), a shuffle (gather by city), and a reduce (sum).

27.3.1 Demo 5 — the same answer, both ways

import pandas as pd

rows = [r for chunk in sales_chunks for r in chunk]
df = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=["city", "amount"])

print("pandas groupby:")
print(df.groupby("city")["amount"].sum().to_dict())

print("\nthe same answer with explicit map-shuffle-reduce steps:")
manual_groups = {}
for chunk in sales_chunks:
    for city, amount in chunk:
        if city not in manual_groups:
            manual_groups[city] = []
        manual_groups[city].append(amount)

manual_totals = {}
for city, amounts in manual_groups.items():
    manual_totals[city] = sum(amounts)

print(manual_totals)

pandas groupby:
{'Delhi': 340, 'Mumbai': 670, 'Pune': 340}

the same answer with explicit map-shuffle-reduce steps:
{'Mumbai': 670, 'Delhi': 340, 'Pune': 340}


The difference is only where the work happens. Pandas does all three steps in one process on one machine. A cluster does the map and reduce on many machines and moves data between them for the shuffle.
